  [1mA. Single namespaced JSON blob, versioned.[0m One key — [38;5;153mwebapp:prefs[39m — holding [38;5;153m{"v": 1, "values": {...}}[39m. A
  [38;5;153mpreferences.js[39m module owns a [38;5;153mDEFAULTS[39m table listing every known preference; on load it parses the blob once, drops
  unknown keys, merges what's left over the defaults, and caches in memory. Writes serialize the whole blob. [3mBuys:[0m
  atomic writes (preferences are never half-updated), one parse, a version field that makes migration a real option
  later, and one-line [38;5;153mreset()[39m. [3mCosts:[0m two tabs writing simultaneously clobber each other's whole blob, not just the key
  in conflict.

  [1mB. One [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m key per preference.[0m [38;5;153mwebapp:pref:rememberUsername[39m, [38;5;153mwebapp:pref:theme[39m, and so on. [3mBuys:[0m per-key
  isolation, so concurrent tabs only conflict on the same preference; partial reads are trivial. [3mCosts:[0m no atomicity
  across related settings, defaults get scattered, every value needs its own encode/decode, and migration or [38;5;153mreset()[39m
  means prefix-scanning the whole [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m keyspace. Versioning has nowhere natural to live.

  [1mC. Observable store with change subscriptions.[0m Either layout above, plus [38;5;153msubscribe(key, fn)[39m and
  [38;5;153mwindow.addEventListener("storage", ...)[39m so consumers react to changes and tabs stay in sync. [3mBuys:[0m a settings UI would
  stay consistent for free, and multi-tab is correct by construction. [3mCosts:[0m listener lifecycle and event plumbing
  built now, for one preference, with no settings panel to justify it.

  [1mI recommend A.[0m The atomicity and the version field both start paying the moment there's a second preference, and the
  multi-tab clobbering it accepts is a non-issue for a page whose only state is one login form. C is the right shape
  [3meventually[0m, and A doesn't block it: if the module's API stays narrow — [38;5;153mget[39m/[38;5;153mset[39m/[38;5;153mreset[39m over a [38;5;153mDEFAULTS[39m table — adding
  subscriptions later is additive, not a rewrite. That's the specific thing the design will protect. B I'd avoid; it
  trades away exactly the properties (atomic writes, a place to put a schema version) that make the second preference
  cheap.
